eparing for travel\nInternational Orientation\nCurrent international students\nInternational orientation\nStudentsafe Inbound University insurance\nVisa support\nStudy support\nContact International Student Experience\nHealth and safety\nRe-enrolling\nManaaki New Zealand Scholarships programme support\nHome\nInternational\nApply and enrol\nEntry requirements\nEnglish Language requirements\nProgrammes with higher English requirements\nInternational\nTe Kahupapa ki Tai / International\nStudy options\nUndergraduate degrees\nPostgraduate programmes\nYour postgraduate study options\nPhD\nStudy Abroad and Exchange\nFoundation Studies\nPathways\nNorthern Consortium UK\nEnglish for Academic Purposes\nWhy study in Wellington\nGraduate employability\nConnect with Wellington\nLiving in Wellington\nDiscover Wellington\nExplore your campuses\nWorking in New Zealand\nMoney and banking\nHealthcare\nBringing your family\nSupport for international students\nVisa and insurance support\nArrival meeting service\nInternational buddy programme\nHealth, safety, and wellbeing\nStudy support\nStudents under 18 years\nInformation sessions\nHello Wellington\u2014India roadshow\nInternational stories\nVisit us in Wellington\nScholarships and fees\nInternational Scholarships\nInternational Scholarships update\nManaaki New Zealand Scholarships Programme\nYour Scholarship experience\nWhat the Scholarship covers\nApply for a Manaaki New Zealand Scholarship\nScholarship study options\nGet ready for your Scholarship\nOrganise your travel\nWhen you first arrive\nMeet our Scholarships team\nFunding for international study\nUS Federal Student Aid\nEligibility\nLoan types\nApplication process\nCost of attendance\nSatisfactory Academic Progress\nReturn of Title IV Funds\nManage and repay your loan\nStudent consumer information\nGlossary\nSallie Mae private loans\nVeterans Affairs Education Benefits\nCost calculator\nYour fees and costs\nPaying your fees\nHow to pay your fees\nApply and enrol\nEntry requirements\nEnglish Language requirements\nStandard undergraduate English requirements\nStandard postgraduate English requirements\nProgrammes with higher English requirements\nProgrammes with external English requirements\nDates and deadlines\nHow to apply\nPrepare your documents\nApply as a New Zealand secondary school student\nP\u016baha\u2014log in or sign up\nYour Offer\nEnrol for courses\nChoosing your courses\nCredit transfer and exemptions\nFind an agent\nApplication and enrolment terms and conditions\nPrepare to study\nYour offer\nEnrol for courses\nDates and deadlines\nStudent visa\nAccommodation\nAccommodation for Scholarship students\nInsurance\nStudentsafe University insurance\nInternational buddy programme\nCheck your health needs\nCourse planning\nPreparing for travel\nArrival meeting service\nInternational Orientation\nOrientation and start dates\nTimetable\nCurrent international students\nInternational orientation\nStudentsafe Inbound University insurance\nVisa support\nStudy support\nContact International Student Experience\nHealth and safety\nRe-enrolling\nManaaki New Zealand Scholarships programme support\nProgrammes with higher English requirements\nFind out the English language criteria for postgraduate programmes with higher requirements.\nEnglish language requirement per degree\nTo study the degrees listed below at Te Herenga Waka\u2014Victoria University of Wellington, you need to show that your English is strong enough. There are several options for meeting this requirement.\nMaster of Laws\nYou can meet the English-language requirement with one of the following:\nIELTS (Academic) 7.0 with no band less than 6.5\nTOEFL IBT 100 with a minimum of 21 in Writing\nPTE overall score of 65 with no communicative skills score below 58\nDuolingo overall DET 130 with no band less than 120\nEAP at VUW Final scores: Reading 5, Writing 5, Speaking 5, Listening 4.\nPhD in Law\nYou can meet the English-language requirement with one of the following:\nIELTS (Academic) 7.0 with 6.5 in Reading, Listening and Speaking, and 7.0 in Writing\nTOEFL IBT 100 with 25 in Writing\nDuolingo overall DET 130 with no band less than 120 and Writing no less than 130\nEAP at VUW Final scores: Reading 5, Writing 5, Speaking 5, Listening 4.\nPhD in Education\nYou can meet the English-language requirement with one of the following:\nIELTS (Academic) 6.5 with no band less than 6.5\nTOEFL IBT 90 with 21 in Writing and other sub-scores at a minimum at the mid-point of high-intermediate level\nDuolingo overall DET 120 with no band less than 120\nEAP at VUW Final scores: 4, 4, 5, 5.\nPhD in Linguistics/Applied Linguistics\nYou can meet the English-language requirement with one of the following:\nIELTS (Academic) 7.0 with no band less than 6.5\nTOEFL IBT\u202f5\u202fwith a minimum of\u202f4.5\u202fin Writing\nPTE overall score of\u202f76\u202fwith no communicative skills score below\u202f70\nDuolingo overall DET 130 with no band less than 120\nEAP at VUW Final scores: Reading 5, Writing 5, Speaking 5, Listening 4.\nHow to share your Duolingo test results\nTo send your Duolingo English Test results to the University, follow the steps in How do I send my Duolingo English Test results to institutions?\nEnglish-speaking countries and English-medium education\nThe evidence you need to provide for your English-language ability depends on your nationality and where you have studied.\nList A countries\nNationals\nIf you are a national of a List A country, you are generally exempt from providing additional evidence of your English-language ability.\nAdditional evidence may be requested if there are concerns about your English ability.\nNon-nationals\nIf you are not a national of a List A country but have studies there, you are generally exempt from providing additional evidence of your English-language ability if you have successfully completed the entire undergraduate or postgraduate degree at a recognised institution in a List A country.\nAdditional evidence may be requested if there are concerns about your English ability.\nList A countries\nAustralia\nBahamas\nBarbados\nCanada (except Quebec)\nCook Islands\nFiji\nIreland\nJamaica\nKenya\nMalta\nMauritius\nNew Zealand\nNigeria\nNiue\nSingapore\nTanzania\nTokelau\nTrinidad and Tobago\nUnited Kingdom\nUSA\nZambia\nZimbabwe\nList B countries\nStudents who studied in a List B country (nationals or non-nationals of that country) may satisfy the English language requirements, if the following criteria are met:\nSuccessful completion of the entire undergraduate or postgraduate degree at a recognised institution from that country\nThe entire content was taught in English\nThe study was completed within two years prior to application\nEnglish as the language of instruction is confirmed on the transcript or with a supporting letter from the institution.\nList B countries\nAntigua and Barbuda\nBelize\nBotswana\nCuba\nCyprus\nDenmark\nDominica\nDominican Republic\nGhana\nGrenada\nGuyana\nHaiti\nHong Kong\nKiribati\nLesotho\nMalaysia\nMarshall Islands\nMicronesia\nNauru\nNetherlands\nNorway\nPalau\nPapua New Guinea\nPhilippines\nSaint Kitts and Nevis\nSaint Lucia\nSaint Vincent and the Grenadines\nSamoa\nSierra Leone\nSolomon Islands\nSouth Africa\nSweden\nTonga\nTuvalu\nUganda\nVanuatu\nWaiver of English Language Requirements\nIf you have studied in one of the countries listed above but do not meet the exemption criteria, you may still be eligible for a waiver of the English language requirements. To be considered, you must provide documented evidence of strong English proficiency. This can include writing samples, reference letters from previous lecturers, or other relevant proof. Waiver requests are assessed individually by the University on a case-by-case basis.\nConnect with us\nContact us\nFind an agent\nInformation sessions\nGet a student guide\nEmergencies\nFaculties\nArchitecture and Design Innovation\nBusiness and Government\nEducation, Health, and Psychological Sciences\nGraduate Research\nHumanities and Social Sciences\nLaw\nScience and Engineering\nContacts and directories\nStaff directory\nMedia enquiries\nFind a researcher\nHealth and safety\nCampuses\nKelburn Campus\nPipitea Campus\nTe Aro Campus\nAuckland Premises\nStudy\nProgrammes and courses\nApply and enrol\nScholarships\nAccommodation\nSchools hub\nStudents\nLibrary\nServices and support\nFinancial survival\nKey dates\nCourses\nP\u016baha\u2014Student portal\nNuku\u2014Learning environment\nLibrary\nStaff\nStaff email\nStaff Service Centre\nMyTools for staff\nTeaching support\nWorking here\nCurrent vacancies\nAlumni\nStay connected\nGiving\nDonate\nLatest news\nEvents\nResearch expertise\nPublications\nSocial media\n0800 04 04 04\n+64 4 472 1000\ninfo@vuw.ac.nz\nEmergency: 0800 842 8888\nSite info\nSite map\nFeedback\nGlossary\nPrivacy\nCopyright \u00a9 Victoria University of Wellington, New Zealand", "links": [["", "javascript:;"], ["", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/international/applying/entry-requirements/english-language/higher/_edit"], ["", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/"], ["Te Kahupapa ki Tai International", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/international"], ["P\u016baha", "https://puaha.wgtn.ac.nz/"], ["Nuku", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/teaching-support/teaching-with-technology/log-in-to-nuku"], ["Staff", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/staff"], ["Apply to study", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/study/apply-enrol"], ["Apply", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/study/apply-enrol"], ["", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/"], ["Te Kahupapa Future students", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/study"], ["Te Kahupapa ki Tai International students", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/international"], ["Ng\u0101 \u0101konga Current students", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/students"], ["Rangahau Research", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/research"], ["Ruawhet\u016b Engage", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/engage"], ["M\u014d m\u0101tou About", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/about"], ["Te P\u0101taka K\u014drero Library", "https://www.wgtn.ac.nz/library"], ["Mapi Maps", "https://w